anges to avoid risk.\\n\\nHow to enable the schedule (pick what matches your OS):\\n- Debian/Ubuntu:\\n  sudo apt-get update &amp;&amp; sudo apt-get install -y cron\\n  (crontab -l 2&gt;/dev/null | grep -v -F \"/root/.local/bin/docker-prune.sh\"; echo \"0 3 * * 0 /root/.local/bin/docker-prune.sh\") | crontab -\\n  sudo service cron start  # or: sudo systemctl enable --now cron\\n\\n- RHEL/CentOS/Fedora:\\n  sudo yum install -y cronie   # or: sudo dnf install -y cronie\\n  (crontab -l 2&gt;/dev/null | grep -v -F \"/root/.local/bin/docker-prune.sh\"; echo \"0 3 * * 0 /root/.local/bin/docker-prune.sh\") | crontab -\\n  sudo systemctl enable --now crond\\n\\n- Alpine:\\n  sudo apk add --no-cache cronie\\n  (crontab -l 2&gt;/dev/null | grep -v -F \"/root/.local/bin/docker-prune.sh\"; echo \"0 3 * * 0 /root/.local/bin/docker-prune.sh\") | crontab -\\n  sudo rc-update add cronie default &amp;&amp; sudo rc-service cronie start\\n\\nHow to test safely now (manual run):\\n- Note: This will actually remove unused images, stopped containers, unused networks, and build cache.\\n  bash /root/.local/bin/docker-prune.sh\\n  tail -n 100 /root/logs/docker-prune.log\\n\\nAdjustments (optional):\\n- Also remove unused volumes (more destructive; data loss possible):\\n  Edit the script and add --volumes to docker system prune:\\n    docker system prune -af --volumes\\n- Change schedule:\\n  Edit your crontab (crontab -e) and adjust the 0 3 * * 0 timing as needed.\\n\\nRollback:\\n- Remove the cron entry:\\n  crontab -l | grep -v -F \"/root/.local/bin/docker-prune.sh\" | crontab -\\n- Delete the script and logs:\\n  rm -f /root/.local/bin/docker-prune.sh\\n  rm -rf /root/logs\\n\\nStatus:\\n- The cleanup script and a suggested weekly cron line are in place.\\n- Cron daemon installation was attempted but appears blocked/hung in this environment; enabling the schedule will require the short steps above on the target host.\\n\\nWould you like me to modify the script to include/exclude volum